  "scope": "Fresh same-evidence hosted S7 briefing; accepted abstention with a measured review priority, not a qualified inspection interval.",
  "interpretation": {
    "focus": "inspection_rationale",
    "answer": {
      "text": "Recommended next step\nPrioritize the integrity engineer's review of pipeline P5 around stations ST3, ST4 and ST5. Both sensors at each station decline together across two shared data connections, with larger changes centrally than at the pipe's ends. This makes P5 a supported investigation priority for a possible physical pressure event, not a confirmed leak. A qualified physical inspection interval remains withheld; these stations are a review focus, not a leak boundary, consistent with KN-LOCALIZATION-R1.\n\nWhat the readings show\nDuring 90-94 seconds elapsed in the monitored recording, P5 ST3-ST5 pressures were approximately 0.18-0.30 bar below their earlier typical pressure; bar is a pressure unit. In the final one-second period, 94-95 seconds, the reductions reached approximately 0.43-0.62 bar. P5's end stations changed less, and the compared P4 and P6 locations showed much smaller changes. The final period contains only one reading per sensor, so the recording cannot establish whether the decline continued.\n\nWhy this matters\nThe observed patterns warrant different investigations rather than one explanation for the whole network. P2 pressures rose approximately 0.35-0.43 bar during 90-94 seconds. P7's two sensors at each of ST1, ST3, ST5 and ST7 fell approximately 0.72-0.84 bar, while intervening stations stayed near their earlier pressure. That every-other-station pattern contrasts with P5's central decline across consecutive stations. The operating and calibration records discussed below offer competing explanations, but do not establish P5's cause.\n\nOther instruments to check\nTreat P7's eight odd-station readings as suspect for a shared measurement offset, not eight independently confirmed faulty sensors. Review P3-ST2-A on pipeline P3: it fell approximately 0.95 bar while the second sensor at that station remained nearly unchanged. P2-ST2-A on pipeline P2 also needs review because consecutive readings differed by 2.93 bar despite its broader upward pressure profile. Overall usable sample coverage was 99.45%, but the health review was partial. High aggregate coverage does not establish every instrument's accuracy, as KN-QUALITY-R1 explains.\n\nWhat would resolve the remaining question\nRequest longer synchronized P5 records, independent pressure and flow measurements, and incident-time operating records. These read-only checks would test whether the central decline persists and has an independent physical explanation. Independent pressure references and calibration outcomes would help assess P7's offset and P3's paired disagreement before attributing sensor faults or proposing a physical inspection interval.",
      "references": [
        "subsea_41b5e48f30c44994898ee7e06bce11e0",
        "subsea_076c06d51f9842afbb998fb7cfc0cbb5",
